answer.text:
The circuit is a coupled LC quadrature oscillator. Its oscillation frequency is mainly set by the LC tank resonance, but the quadrature-coupling current \(I_1\) shifts it slightly away from the natural tank frequency.

Let

\[
\omega_0=\frac{1}{\sqrt{LC_T}}, \qquad 
f_0=\frac{1}{2\pi\sqrt{LC_T}}
\]

where \(C_T\) is the total tank capacitance at each LC node, including MOS parasitics, varactors/load capacitance, etc.

For a tank quality factor \(Q\), and assuming the coupling pair current is \(I_1\) while the main oscillator tail current is \(I_{SS}\), the oscillation frequency is approximately

\[
\boxed{
\frac{\omega_{\text{osc}}}{\omega_0}
\approx
\sqrt{1+\left(\frac{I_1}{2QI_{SS}}\right)^2}
\;\pm\;
\frac{I_1}{2QI_{SS}}
}
\]

or, for weak coupling,

\[
\boxed{
f_{\text{osc}} \approx f_0
\left(1 \pm \frac{I_1}{2QI_{SS}}\right)
}
\]

The sign depends on the coupling polarity/phase sequence. If the coupling is weak or the tank \(Q\) is high, the shift is small, so a practical estimate is simply

\[
\boxed{
f_{\text{osc}} \approx \frac{1}{2\pi\sqrt{LC_T}}
}
\]
